#!/bin/sh
#
# A two-line shim, and the reason is worth knowing before you "simplify" it.
#
# The first version of this hook was a real shell script using wc, tr and grep.
# It could not run on the development machine: git there is **MinGit**, a
# stripped-down Git for Windows that ships usr/bin/sh.exe but none of the MSYS
# userland, so every one of those utilities is missing. A hook that cannot
# execute is worse than no hook, because it looks installed.
#
# So the logic lives in Node, which this project already requires at >=20 and
# already uses for three scripts/ checks. `exec node` is the only thing this
# file needs from the shell. The relative path is safe: git runs hooks with the
# working directory set to the top of the repository.
#
# Install once per clone (git does not run tracked hooks on its own):
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# Bypass for one commit:  git commit --no-verify
exec node .githooks/pre-commit.mjs
